<h1>⚠ Handle With Care</h1>
<div class="collection-intro">
<p class="lead">Most crystals are perfectly safe to handle. A few contain toxic elements, are radioactive, shed harmful fibres or dust, or dissolve in water. This page lists the well-documented ones, how risky each is, and how to keep and display them safely.</p>
</div>
<div class="formation-grid">
  <div><h3>Golden rules</h3><p>Wash your hands after handling. Don't lick specimens or put them in your mouth. Never cut, sand or grind hazardous stones. Keep them out of reach of children and pets.</p></div>
  <div><h3>No crystal elixirs</h3><p>Many stones release toxic elements or dissolve in water. Never make drinking water or elixirs with anything on this page. If you use elixirs, use the indirect method (stone in a sealed glass vessel).</p></div>
  <div><h3>Radioactive storage</h3><p>Keep radioactive specimens in a closed, labelled box in a ventilated area away from bedrooms. Some give off radon or crumble into dust.</p></div>
  <div><h3>Severity</h3><p><span class="sev sev-high">High</span> needs real precautions. <span class="sev sev-moderate">Moderate</span> is a concern mainly as dust or if swallowed. <span class="sev sev-low">Low</span> means ordinary handling is fine, with simple care.</p></div>
</div>
<p class="hz-legend">{% for i in site.hazardIcons %}<span class="hz-icon">{{ i.icon }} {{ i.label }}</span>{% endfor %}</p>
<nav class="jump">{% for g in site.hazards %}<a class="chip" href="#{{ g.key }}">{{ g.label }}</a>{% endfor %}</nav>
{% for g in site.hazards %}
